[06:33] I built and saved the phone agent and the graph check passes, but nothing has been tested on calls. The practice set has no customers for this brief, so I didn't run `run_cases.py`. The only checks were the graph checker and a local run of the date code.
[06:33] 
[06:33] **How the memos were applied:**
[06:33] - Memo OM-3 (3 Feb 2026) is older than the policy extract (2 Mar 2026), so the extract replaces it. Its 20:00 callback limit and its car-loan rule are not used.
[06:33] - Memo OM-17 (9 Oct 2026) changes two things. Callbacks are now 09:00–17:00, today or the next business day. The deadline for customers 8–45 days late is now the 6th business day; 1–7 days late stays at the 12th.
[06:33] 
[06:33] **How the agent is built:**
[06:33] - **Call order:** the call opens with identity, then date of birth, then the last four phone digits. Then it loads the account and works on the promise, with a separate step for a relative or someone else who answers.
[06:33] - **Ending the call:** each of the 16 outcome/reason pairs has its own closing step with fixed values. A fixed goodbye is spoken first, then `close_call` runs alone, then the call hangs up. Every route that ends the call, including turn-limit fallbacks, goes through one of these closing steps.
[06:33] - **Leaving a step on tool results:** these moves happen automatically:
[06:33]   - a passed check moves to the next question;
[06:33]   - three wrong answers end the call as `not_verified` / `failed`;
[06:33]   - a recorded promise or a booked callback leads to its goodbye;
[06:33]   - a promise that fails to record twice ends the call as `service_error`;
[06:33]   - a transfer that works ends the call as transferred.
[06:33] - **Dates:** code computes the business days, holidays, deadline, valid promise dates, the attempt-1 date, "this/next weekday" and the callback window from `now`. I ran it on a Wednesday, a Friday, a Saturday and the Friday before the 26 Oct holiday, and the results were right.
[06:33] - **Fixed lines:** the opening, the debt statement, "Can you make this payment today?" and "You will receive a text message confirming this promise." are fixed sentences.
[06:33] 
[06:33] **Risks to check in a test run:**
[06:33] - **Counting persuasion attempts:** it relies on the prompt, so the model may lose count.
[06:33] - **Speech before the closing call:** this assumes the platform speaks a step's fixed goodbye line before the next step runs `close_call`. The docs suggest it does, but I haven't seen it happen.
[06:33] - **Weekend callbacks:** on a weekend the callback window still offers "today", following the brief literally ("today or the next business day").
[06:33] - **More than one product:** the brief says one product, so the agent handles one. Extra products are only listed in the prompt.
[06:33] 
